n pressure is the upstream cause (database), giving the decisive discriminator for the next decision.\", \"assignments\": [{\"task_id\": \"t-0001-logs-symptom-window\", \"role\": \"logs\", \"question\": \"Within the observation window, which services (gateway, backend, postgres, redis, payment) emit ERROR/CRITICAL log signatures, and what is the earliest timestamp and error content per service?\", \"allowed_tools\": [\"query_logs\"], \"filters\": {\"service\": \"backend\", \"severity\": \"ERROR\", \"start_ts\": \"2026-10-01T11:30:00Z\", \"end_ts\": \"2026-10-01T12:30:00Z\", \"limit\": 50}, \"reason\": \"Establishes the chronological ordering of error signatures across the service list before attributing a culprit; symptom onset order is required to separate an upstream cause from downstream symptoms.\", \"expected_discriminator\": \"If backend logs show pool/connection or timeout errors that begin before gateway errors appear, the datastore/backend path is upstream; if only gateway/payment errors appear while backend and datastore stay clean, the failure is confined to the edge/external path.\"}, {\"task_id\": \"t-0002-db-contention-and-connections\", \"role\": \"database\", \"question\": \"During the observation window, does postgres exhibit connection saturation, lock waits, or slow queries that precede the observed symptom timing?\", \"allowed_tools\": [\"get_db_connections\", \"get_db_active_sessions\", \"get_db_lock_waits\", \"get_db_slow_queries\", \"get_db_health_metrics\"], \"filters\": {\"service\": \"postgres\", \"start_ts\": \"2026-10-01T11:30:00Z\", \"end_ts\": \"2026-10-01T12:30:00Z\", \"limit\": 50}, \"reason\": \"Distinguishes a datastore-side mechanism (connection exhaustion or lock contention) from an application-side pool regression or resource saturation; the two predict different observable signatures at the database layer.\", \"expected_discriminator\": \"Elevated lock waits or slow queries point to lock contention/slow query mechanisms, exhausted connection counts point to connection exhaustion, and a clean database layer with app-side pool errors points instead to a backend pool config regression.\"}], \"hypothesis_updates\": [], \"selected_hypothesis_id\": null, \"stop_reason\": null}, {\"action\": \"INVESTIGATE\", \"reason\": \"Round 1 with the review verdict 'insufficient' for the only OPEN candidate hyp-2354bf4d3f473cedacf9117199ff63a5928dab8fb231824dd4f90ea588ae234e (backend_pool_config_regression); the alternative hyp-51bd04666262ef67528352206d8217fe26919d211c65863ee2740d9edeef0a3b (postgres_connection_exhaustion) is already REJECTED and cannot be finalized. Observed evidence covers only a benign gateway completion (incident_1002-E-002), a mechanism-free backend deadline symptom (incident_1002-E-008), and postgres at 30 of 100 connections (incident_1002-E-007), so no cause-specific mechanism has been witnessed. Budget (42 remaining requests, 27 tool calls, 7 rounds, 20 affordable tasks) afford investigation. Dispatch the two decisive requested checks that supply the missing mechanism: the backend pool-related config/deployment diff (no change record has ever been queried) and the backend application-side pool/connection error logs (the second independent source required for sufficiency).\", \"assignments\": [{\"task_id\": \"t-0004-backend-pool-config-diff\", \"role\": \"changes\", \"question\": \"Between 2026-10-01T11:30:00Z and 2026-10-01T12:30:00Z, were any deployment or configuration changes applied to the backend service that altered connection-pool size, acquire timeout, or database connection settings, and what do the retrieved config diffs show?\", \"allowed_tools\": [\"query_changes\", \"get_config_diff\"], \"filters\": {\"service\": \"backend\", \"start_ts\": \"2026-10-01T11:30:00Z\", \"end_ts\": \"2026-10-01T12:30:00Z\", \"change_type\": \"config\"}, \"reason\": \"backend_pool_config_regression requires an observed config mechanism preceding the 2026-10-01T12:00:14Z symptom, and no change